# Database Security Roles

Two PostgreSQL roles exist, provisioned by
`infra/docker/postgres/init/01-create-roles.sh` (see also
`infra/docker/postgres/init/templates/roles.template.sql`):

- **`migrator_role`** — schema owner. Performs migrations, DDL, schema changes,
  and RLS policy creation. Used only by developer tooling / CI migration jobs
  (e.g. `dotnet ef database update`).
- **`app_role`** — used by the running ASP.NET Core application at runtime.

## `app_role` must never

- Own the database or any schema.
- Have `BYPASSRLS`.
- Have DDL rights (`CREATE`, `ALTER`, `DROP`) beyond what a migration explicitly
  grants per table (`SELECT`/`INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE`).
- Be a superuser.

## Never run the application as `migrator_role` or a PostgreSQL superuser

The connection string used by `PTS.Host` (and any future worker process) must use
`app_role` credentials only. Migration tooling uses `migrator_role` credentials via
a separate connection string/config, never the application's runtime config.

## When adding a new tenant-owned table

A migration authored to run as `migrator_role` must, at minimum:

1. Create the table with a non-nullable `TenantId` column.
2. `ALTER TABLE <table> ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;`
3. `ALTER TABLE <table> FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;` (so even the table owner is
   subject to the policy).
4. Create a policy scoping rows to `current_setting('app.current_tenant_id')`.
5. `GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON <table> TO app_role;` — explicitly,
   never via a blanket `ALL ON ALL TABLES` grant.
